Fix the char-boundary panic in fast_parse_date

`src/metadata/mod.rs` tests for a bare `YYYYMMDD` date by taking `s[..8]` behind
a `s.len() >= 8` guard. `len()` counts bytes and the slice indexes bytes, but a
multi-byte character straddling byte 8 makes that slice split it, and slicing a
`&str` off a char boundary panics:

    <meta property="article:published_time" content="1234567é9">
    end byte index 8 is not a char boundary; it is inside 'é' (bytes 7..9)

The value comes from a meta tag in an untrusted document, so this is reachable
by anyone who can choose the page we are asked to extract.

Comparing the first eight *bytes* against ASCII digits is what the surrounding
code already means: the three slices inside the branch — `s[..4]`, `s[4..6]`,
`s[6..8]` — are only sound because those bytes are digits, which the check now
establishes directly. Behaviour is otherwise unchanged. A string whose first
eight bytes are not all ASCII digits took the false branch before and takes it
now; the only difference is that it no longer panics on the way there.

Ours, not upstream's — nobody had reported this as of 2026-07-27. Reporting it
is https://github.com/bravely/ex_trafilatura/issues/40; this patch does not wait
on that.

